+{"seq": 21, "ts": 1791975989.764, "role": "assistant", "content": "Confirmed: the shell sandbox has **network=off** (`wget: bad address`), so `web_crawl` is the only network path — and it fetched the page (HTTP 200, 6657 bytes) but stores only metadata, not the body. Before declaring \"data unavailable,\" let me check local files for hints/cached data and try a delegate with fresh tools.\n\n[STATUS]\nDONE: Ruled out shell fetch (no network); web_crawl reaches Yahoo but discards response body\nNEXT: Check local metadata/memories + delegate a fetch attempt\nBLOCKERS: no tool currently returns HTTP body content", "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: the shell sandbox has network=off (\"wget: bad address\" — DNS fails). Only the web_crawl tool has network access, and it stores only metadata (status, bytes, links, forms), not the response body.\n\nSo the situation: web_crawl CAN reach Yahoo (200, 6657 bytes) but I cannot read the body through any tool I have. Let me think about whether there's any other way to extract the body:\n\n1. `web_crawl` with different params — the artifact format is fixed (crawl.json + meta.json). The body is not saved.\n2. Maybe crawling a URL whose content gets reflected into \"links\" or \"forms\"? The chart API returns JSON; links/forms arrays are empty. No.\n3. Could I use a URL that echoes data into a form? No.\n4. `delegate` — spawns a sub-agent with read-only tools. Same toolset, same limitation. It could try web_crawl too, but the body still wouldn't be readable. Actually wait — maybe the sub-agent has different tools? \"fresh context, read-only tools\" — likely the same read-only set. Not promising, but it's a possible avenue. Actually, the sub-agent's summary could include... no, it can't read the body either.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe web_crawl's artifact DOES include page content in some other location, or maybe there's a \"sessions/\" dir with the full crawl. Let me check sessions/ and agent_out.log. Also run_meta.json an